            <div xml:id="d748-a1">

                <pb facs="0834" n="754" sameAs="frus:frus1902/pg754"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. McCreery to Mr.
                            Mariscal.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Embassy of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1900-06-14"><hi rendition="#italic">June 14,
                        1900</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Minister</hi>: Referring to the ambassador’s note
                    of December 19, 1899, this embassy has the honor to bring again to the attention
                    of your excellency’s Government the claim known as “The Pious Fund of the
                    Californias” against Mexico.</p>
                <p>The Government of the United States regards this claim as undoubtedly just, its
                    essential justice having been already established by one arbitral hearing and
                    decision, having the proper effect of res judicata. Merignhac, L’Arbitrage
                    International, section 306, speaking of the arbitral sentence, says:</p>
                <p>“The sentence, duly given within the limits of the convention, decides the
                    question between the parties in a definitive manner (art. 25 of the regulation
                    of the Institute); it therefore has the authority of chose jugee, like judgments
                    rendered in the last resort by the ordinary tribunals of a country.”</p>
                <p>I have the honor to renew to your excellency the assurance of my most
                    distinguished consideration.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Fenton R.
                    McCreery.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
